Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["Simple Markov model", "Title",ExpressionUUID->"d073459d-127b-439c-b315-94de6963b699"],

Cell["\<\
This reservoir architecture is a single delay whose input merges the token \
with its own permuted output. The rate_limit of 6.0 is relative to the input \
population of 10, so the limit is 60 elements. Because the limit acts on the \
merged input, the new token is subsampled together with the feedback.

In steady state, 10 fresh elements plus 60 fed back are cut to 60, so every \
element survives a step with probability 6/7. A token k steps back is \
therefore present as about 10\[CenterDot](6/7)^(k+1) elements, all carrying \
the permutation \[Pi]^k.

So the state is one bundle of the recent tokens. Each \
token\[CloseCurlyQuote]s position is coded by the depth of its permutation, \
and its recency by how many of its elements are left. Forgetting is \
stochastic and geometric, with a mean lifetime of 7 tokens; there are no \
boundaries and no explicit order k. The predictor stores every (state \
\[RightArrow] next token) pair and matches by overlap. Whatever part of the \
history is discriminative carries the match, so the circuit behaves as a \
soft, variable-order Markov model. The pair counts are the evidence: on \
random strings over 26 letters, 2 to 3 tokens of context are already unique, \
and that is why it reaches 1.0.\
\>", "Text",ExpressionUUID->"86cc569a-be37-4aff-8a57-73ac76739a7f"],

